1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Welcome.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,000
This is Task 1, problem definition and scope.

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Before writing any code, a project must say what problem it solves and when it counts as finished.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:19,000
That agreement is written in one document inside the project's docs folder.

5
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:21,000
I am listing the numbered documents.

6
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:28,000
The first one, zero one, is our Task 1 document, and it is kept in Git like code.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,000
I am opening the problem statement.

8
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:37,000
A small hotel with six rooms takes bookings by phone and spreadsheet.

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Guests cannot see free rooms, and sometimes two people get the same room for the same night.

10
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,000
That is the business problem.

11
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:57,000
The second paragraph is the DevOps problem: changes must reach guests quickly without breaking bookings, so we replace manual copying and testing with an automated pipeline.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Target users are the people who use the system directly: the guest, the developer and the operator.

13
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Stakeholders are everyone affected by it, such as the hotel owner and the front desk.

14
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Each wants something different.

15
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:20,000
The owner wants no double bookings, the operator wants safe deployments.

16
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Please note that these are a reasoned model of a small hotel.

17
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,000
We did not interview real hotel staff.

18
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,000
These are the six existing pain points, from double bookings to having no rollback after a bad release.

19
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Each objective, O1 to O7, answers them.

20
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:52,000
For example O2 says overlapping bookings must be impossible, both in code and in the database, and O4 says a failing test must block deployment automatically.

21
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Constraints are the limits we work within.

22
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:04,000
Technically we use Python, Flask and SQLite, which allows one writer at a time.

23
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:13,000
Operationally we have a ten day schedule, no paid cloud, and no public server, so Jenkins polls Git instead of using a webhook.

24
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Stating constraints early explains design choices later.

25
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Success criteria are targets you can check with a number or a yes or no.

26
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:32,000
The left column is the target, the right column is the evidence we achieved it.

27
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:39,000
For example, at least fifty automated tests all passing, and a repeat Ansible run that changes nothing.

28
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:42,000
Saying it works well cannot be proven.

29
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:44,000
Ninety of ninety tests passing can.

30
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:52,000
Let me prove one criterion live instead of just reading it.

31
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:57,000
I am running the project's automated tests, without the browser tests.

32
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:06,000
If every test passes, the criterion that the tests must all pass is met right now, not only on the day the document was written.

33
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:09,000
This takes about twenty seconds.

34
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Finally the scope.

35
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:19,000
MVP means Minimum Viable Product, the smallest version that is genuinely useful.

36
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:29,000
Ours is six booking workflows: register and log in, check availability by date, request, confirm, cancel, and track a booking.

37
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:37,000
Payments, an admin screen and e-mails are deliberately out of scope, so the project stays finishable.

38
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:44,000
What I tell the professor: the problem is clear, the scope is fixed, and success is measured.


